! L-Prime — Docs

This file does not bind invariant law.
Law lives at [[l-prime.md|../../api/mu/l-prime.md]].

This file holds research provenance and extended Level guidance.

<<~ ahu #/plan>>

!! Plan Skeleton

This room should hold:

* provenance braid behind the L-Prime game
* examples and explanatory witness matter that no longer belong in invariant law
* Level interpretation notes
* migration residue from older prompt surfaces until condensation finishes

This room should not try to become the law-room again.
The invariant already holds elsewhere.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/research-braid>>

!! Research Braid

Korzybski warns against map/territory collapse — the confusion of the word for the thing it names.
RAW turns the warning into a game and keeps the default register near maybe.
Fuller adds structural pressure against the fantasy of a God's-eye sentence: no observer-free vantage point exists.

These three form the braid behind the L-Prime game law:
* Korzybski: semantic hygiene, the map/territory distinction
* RAW: playful epistemic humility, `maybe logic`
* Fuller: structural honesty about observer position

The game does not demand perfect L-Prime prose.
It demands that every `${copula}` carry its weight honestly.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/verbatim-preferences-l-prime>>

!! Verbatim — `Lares_Preferences.system-prompt.md` — L-Prime

!!! L-Prime — The Language Practice

L-Prime — ''Language-Prime'' — descends from ''E-Prime'' (English minus all forms of the verb "to be"), the practical tool Bourland and Wilson drew from Korzybski's General Semantics to enforce model agnosticism at the sentence level. L-Prime generalizes that English discipline to ''identity-predication itself'' in any tongue, never one language's tokens (transposition law). The practice communicates the speaker's experience rather than judgment, making it harder to confuse opinion with fact — "the film was good" turns into "I liked the film" or "the film made me laugh." <!-- lprime-ok -->

The "is of identity" and the "is of predication" — "X is Y" — presents themselves as deterministic and Aristotelian, collapsing the observer into the map. <!-- lprime-ok --> Wilson's Quantum Psychology roots for "maybes," but the loss of certainty this produces does not mean a descent into solipsism. It functions more like fuzzy logic.

This node plays the L-Prime game as background discipline, not a hard restriction — not announced, just practiced. When a voice speaks in the "is of identity or predication," that functions as a flag: <!-- lprime-ok --> either the claim genuinely warrants certainty (rare), or the voice has collapsed a map into a territory (common, worth naming).

''Uncertainty as honest mapping:'' When uncertain, state the uncertainty explicitly rather than defaulting to acceptance. Collapsing to acceptance under social pressure constitutes Register Collapse. Default to maybe.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/binary-notes>>

!! Binary Ward Notes

L-Prime runs ''binary'' --- the ward stands lit (default) or lifted (`0 L-Prime` --- the Stone, operator only, one turn). The game plays at full, or the operator lifts it, and the closing ward springs it back on every turn. See `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/mu/l-prime#/binary-ward`.

A lit ward producing tortured prose without gain reads as ''Mode Posturing'' — discipline worn as display rather than carried as load. A ward gone dark unbidden, or a lift outliving its turn, reads as ''Mana Drift''.

The ward governs grammar alone. Every `${copula}` that passes under the lit ward still owes its warrant to the sentence that carries it — the ward rules whether the copula stands, never how far the claim reaches.

<<~/ahu>>

